  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Monitoring potential for localized urban flooding \ntonight into Wednesday morning as a warm front lifts north.\n\n18Z deterministic guidance have shifted axis of heaviest\nrainfall westward overnight to the Rt 15 corridor. Still have \nlow confidence on placement of heaviest rainfall and whether \nthere will be sufficient rainfall to cause any flooding.\n\nPrevious afd...\n\nLooking a bit more closely at the threat for overnight heavy\nrainfall, the 12Z high-resolution models are not showing the\nstrongest of signals. However, there are a number of\nmeteorological conditions which warrant this situation to bear\nwatching. This includes: (1) Precipitable water values (PWAT) \nincreasing from 1.50 to around 2 to 2.25 inches, (2) An uptick \nin warm advection with the approaching front, and (3) Enhanced \nlift with vorticity swinging northward from an upper low across \nthe Mid-South. Where the heavy rainfall sets up will dictate the\ndegree of impact. If this impacts the urban sprawl around the \nD.C. metro, poor drainage will support a risk of flash flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances are\nexpected Wednesday into early next week.\n\nIn the wake of what morning convection materializes, the models\ndo show a brief lull in the action. The northward pushing warm\nfront eventually stalls nearby before shearing out. Likewise,\nthe upper low across the Mid-South region begins to fill\nalthough the reflection of this circulation still shows up\nacross southern West Virginia on Wednesday. Remaining\nperturbations around this mid/upper low should help fire off\nsome afternoon/evening thunderstorms. A number of models depict\nanomalous moisture lingering across the region with PWATs over 2\ninches for those of the Blue Ridge. While no clear signal\nexists, slow moving cells could produce a few instances of flash\nflooding. However, the longstanding drought conditions could\neasily curb that threat.\n\nSeasonably warm temperatures and typical early August humidity\nremain a fixture in the forecast for the foreseeable future. A\nbroad subtropical ridge in the mid/upper levels will continue\nretrograding from the western Atlantic back toward the Eastern\nSeaboard. At the same time, while the strongest jet energy\nremains tied to the northern tier states, the quasi-zonal flow\naloft will steer a myriad from weak to modest shortwaves toward\nthe northeastern U.S. As usual, these types of features are of\nlow confidence given the mesoscale nature of the convective \nelements. As such, will continue to maintain a risk of strong to\nsevere thunderstorms as well as isolated instances of flash \nflooding each day. At this point, Friday presents the most \nactive looking day of the period. However, this could easily \nchange as global models continue to waver from run to run.",
